<GuideLayout
  title="What sleeping mat R-value do you need?"
  seoTitle="Sleeping Pad R-Value Chart: What Camping Mat R-Value You Need"
  description="R-value for sleeping mats and pads explained: what the number means, an R-value chart by season, ASTM F3340 testing, and foam vs self-inflating vs air mats."
  eyebrow="Sleep system"
  readTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>
    Most people blame the sleeping bag when they wake up cold. Often the real
    culprit is underneath them. Your body presses the bag's insulation flat
    wherever you lie on it, so the ground draws heat straight out of your back
    unless the mat stops it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is what R-value measures. Once you know how to read it, choosing a
    camping mat gets much simpler.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        R-value measures how well a mat resists heat flowing from your body into
        the ground. Higher is warmer.
      </li>
      <li>
        Roughly: R1-2 for warm summer nights, R2-4 for most Australian hiking
        outside alpine areas, R4-5+ for alpine areas and nights below freezing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Only ratings tested to ASTM F3340 can be compared directly between
        brands.
      </li>
      <li>R-values add up, so a foam mat under an inflatable boosts warmth.</li>
      <li>
        Your mat and <a href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
          >sleeping bag temperature rating</a
        > work as one system.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What is R-value on a sleeping pad?</h2>
  <p>
    R-value is a measure of thermal resistance: how strongly a material resists
    heat moving through it. On a sleeping pad, a higher R-value means less of
    your body heat is lost into the cold ground.
  </p>
  <p>
    R-value measures insulation alone; comfort and thickness are separate. Some
    thick air mattresses insulate poorly, and some thin insulated mats are warm.
  </p>

  <h2>Sleeping pad R-value chart: what R-value do you need?</h2>
  <p>
    Mat makers and hiking publications broadly agree on these bands. Treat them
    as starting points, then adjust for how warm you sleep.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr
            ><th>R-value</th><th>Typical use</th><th>Australian examples</th
            ></tr
          >
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>1-2</td>
            <td>Warm summer nights</td>
            <td>Summer coastal and tropical camping</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>2-4</td>
            <td>Most Australian hiking</td>
            <td>Spring to autumn, and mild coastal and lowland winters</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4-5</td>
            <td>Cold nights near freezing</td>
            <td>Frosty inland valleys, high country in spring and autumn</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>5+</td>
            <td>Snow camping</td>
            <td>Alpine winter trips</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      General R-value bands by season. ASTM F3340 does not assign exact
      temperatures to R-values.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    For easy spring-to-autumn overnighters at established campsites, R2-3.5 is
    plenty. A foam mat around R2 is fine from late spring to early autumn. The
    higher bands are there for reference if you later head somewhere colder.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you sleep cold, move up a band. If you are pairing a mat with a bag you are
    not sure is warm enough for the forecast, extra R-value is the cheapest
    way to add margin. Small things matter too: wearing a beanie, eating before
    bed and simply being a warm or cold sleeper often make more difference than
    half a point of R-value.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Bringing a pool lilo or an uninsulated car-camping air bed. The air inside
      gets cold and pulls heat out of you all night. Use a foam mat, or put one
      under the air bed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Forgetting that it gets colder near dawn, and that the ground is often
      colder than the air on clear nights.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pitching on bare rock or concrete platforms. Grass or leaf litter is
      warmer underneath you.
    </li>
    <li>
      Blowing up an inflatable with your mouth in the cold. Moisture from your
      breath builds up inside, so use a pump sack if you have one.
    </li>
    <li>
      Inflating the mat early and leaving it. The air cools and shrinks, so top
      it up at bedtime.
    </li>
    <li>Heading out with an inflatable and no patch kit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is the ASTM F3340 R-value standard?</h2>
  <p>
    In short: a tested number can be compared, an untested number needs extra
    margin. If a listing names ASTM F3340, you can compare its R-value with
    other tested mats. If it gives a number with no standard, treat it as a
    claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before ASTM F3340 was published in 2020, every brand tested mats its own
    way. The standard gave everyone the same lab test. Therm-a-Rest, one of the
    brands involved in developing it, <a
      href="https://cascadedesigns.com/blogs/thermarest-gear/rating-sleeping-pad-insulation-astm-r-value-standard"
      rel="noopener">explains the test method here</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many budget mats sold online are untested, including both mats we currently
    review. Treat the claim as a rough starting point, check owner reports from
    similar nights, and bring a warm layer.
  </p>

  <h2>Foam vs self-inflating vs air mats: which is warmest?</h2>

  <h3>Closed-cell foam pad R-value</h3>
  <p>
    Foam mats cannot puncture or deflate, need no pump and are cheap. Their
    R-value is usually modest (often around R2) and they are bulky, so they often
    ride on the outside of a pack; if yours does, keep it in a bag. They also double as a sit pad. The <a
      href="/gear/foil-egg-crate-foam-pad/">foil egg-crate foam mat</a
    > we review claims R2.5 without an ASTM test, and is a dependable choice for mild
    first trips.
  </p>

  <h3>Self-inflating mats</h3>
  <p>
    A self-inflating mat combines open-cell foam with an airtight cover. Open
    the valve and the foam expands, drawing in air. They are comfortable, fairly
    warm for their thickness and more puncture-tolerant than pure air mats, but
    heavier and bulkier than an insulated air mat.
  </p>

  <h3>Insulated air mats</h3>
  <p>
    Inflatable mats with internal insulation or reflective layers give the best
    warmth for their packed size and weight. The trade-offs are cost, puncture
    risk and the need to inflate them each night. The <a
      href="/gear/naturehike-tuye-r3-5/">Naturehike R3.5 mat</a
    > listing sits in this group. Its seller claims R3.5, but the exact model and
    tested insulation have not been confirmed.
  </p>

