  <h2>Can you combine sleeping mats to increase R-value?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes. R-values of stacked mats add together, so a foam mat (say R2) under an
    inflatable (say R3) gives roughly R5. This is how many hikers adapt <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">their setup</a
    > for colder winter nights without buying a new mat. The foam layer also protects the inflatable
    from punctures and gives you a basic backup if it fails overnight. Our <a
      href="/gear/foil-egg-crate-foam-pad/">foil egg-crate foam mat</a
    > under the <a href="/gear/naturehike-tuye-r3-5/">Naturehike R3.5 mat</a> is
    one budget example of this pairing.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    A cheap foam mat under your inflatable is the lowest-cost warmth upgrade in
    hiking.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>How to choose a sleeping mat R-value for hiking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pick an R-value band for the coldest trips you plan.</li>
    <li>Prefer ASTM F3340-tested ratings when comparing brands.</li>
    <li>Check length and width: shoulders and hips should stay on the mat.</li>
    <li>
      Side sleepers generally need more thickness to keep hips off the ground.
    </li>
    <li>Weigh packed size against your pack volume.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not sure where to start? Our <a href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a> pair each
    mat with a matching sleeping bag, and <a href="/build-my-kit/"
      >Build My Kit</a
    > fills gaps in gear you already own.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    R-value is the number that decides whether your mat keeps the ground's cold
    away from you. Aim for about R2-4 for most Australian hiking, go higher for
    cold nights, trust tested ratings over claims, and remember that stacking a
    foam mat is an easy upgrade.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>What is a good R-value for a sleeping pad?</summary>
      <p>
        For most Australian hiking, R2-4 covers the majority of trips. Cold
        sleepers and hikers heading to alpine areas or frosty inland sites
        should look at R4 and above.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is R-value the same for a mat and a sleeping pad?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. "Mat" is the more common Australian term and "pad" the American
        one, but the R-value means the same thing and is measured the same way.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What R-value do I need for winter camping?</summary>
      <p>
        Outside alpine areas, an R3-4 mat, plus a foam mat underneath on colder
        nights, covers most Australian winters. For nights near or below
        freezing, look at around R4-5 or higher, and more for
        snow camping. Stacking a foam mat under an inflatable is a common way to
        get there.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is a higher R-value always better?</summary>
      <p>
        For warmth, yes, but higher R-value mats usually cost and weigh more. On
        warm summer trips a very high R-value adds weight you do not need.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Are foam mats warmer than inflatable mats?</summary>
      <p>
        Not usually. Basic foam mats have modest R-values, while insulated air
        mats are often warmer. Uninsulated air mattresses, though, can be colder
        than foam.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I trust R-values on cheap sleeping mats?</summary>
      <p>
        Treat them with caution unless the listing names ASTM F3340. Untested
        figures can be optimistic, so allow extra margin or add a foam layer.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
